{"title":"Exercise 2","markdown":{"headingText":"Exercise 2","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Recap\n\nLast week we covered the following: \n\n<div class=\"row\">\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Exercise 1 Tasks</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nT1: Downloading and Loading ESS Subset on R. ✅<br>\nT2: Introduction to Key Commands ✅ <br>\nT3: Plotting Variable Distributions <br>\nT4: Cross Country and Mode Comparision  \n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\nBefore continuing from where we left in the Exercise 1, let us have a quick recap exercise! \n\n<div class=\"accordion\" id=\"essExerciseAccordion\">\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepOne\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseOne\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseOne\">\nTask 1.1: Downloading and Loading Data\n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseOne\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepOne\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\nGo to the <a href=\"https://ess.sikt.no/en/data-builder/\" target=\"_blank\">ESS Data Builder</a>, select <b>Round 11</b>, select <b>any 2 countries</b>, and <b>any 5 variables</b> (what ever you find interesting!). Check how they are stored on the <a href=\"https://ess.sikt.no/en/datafile/242aaa39-3bbb-40f5-98bf-bfb1ce53d8ef\" target=\"_blank\">ESS variable guide</a> and also look at the graphs. Download the data and load it into R. \n\nIt should look something like this (But let us try writing the code without the hint first!): \n<br><br>\n<code>library(__________)</code><br>\n<code>library(__________)</code><br>\n<code>ASSIGN_A_NAME <- read____(\"path to file\")</code><br>\n<code>ANOTHER_NAME <- c(____)</code><br>\n<code>ASSIGN_A_NAME <- set____________(ASSIGN_A_NAME, .labels = ________)</code>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepTwo\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseTwo\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseTwo\">\nTask 1.2: Viewing and Exploring the Dataset\n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseTwo\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepTwo\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\nUse these commands to understand the data you just downloaded. It is <b>very important</b> to check the variable types and dimensions:\n<br><br>\n<code>view(data)</code>: Opens full data in another tab.<br>\n<code>glimpse(data)</code>: Dimensions, column names, and datatypes.<br>\n<code>head(data)</code>: Shows top rows.<br>\n<code>tail(data)</code>: Shows bottom rows.<br>\n<code>dim(data)</code>: Shows row and column count.<br>\n<code>colnames(data)</code>: Lists all column names.\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepFour\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseFour\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseFour\">\nTask 1.3: Single Column Calculation\n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseFour\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepFour\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\nDo you remember how to select a column in R? Which **sign** should we use? Take one numeric column from your dataset and check its mean! \n<br><br>\n<code>mean()</code>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<br>\n\n\nLet us go back to Exercise 1 now!\n\n## Task List + Independant Work \n<div class=\"row\">\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">In-Class Task</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nT1: Finishing Recap and Exercise 1<br>\nT2: Splitting Variable of Choice <br>\nT3: Information Shortcut Task<br>\nT4: Constructed preferences\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Independent Work</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nIT1: Completing Independent Tasks from Week 1  <br>\nIT2: Testing all 3 Ways to Split a Column <br>\nIT3: Plotting New Distributions With/Without the Split\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n## Splitting Variables\n\nThere are multiple ways to split a variable in R. Now that we know what ``$`` does, let us look at how to create new columns. To start of, let is create an empty column. \n\n\nTo create an empty column, we will use this command: ``Dataframe_name$Name_of_new_column <- NA``. For example:\n\n```{r,echo=F, message=FALSE}\nlibrary(readr)\nESS_Subset <- read_csv(\"~/Downloads/ESS11e04_1-subset (2)/ESS11e04_1-subset.csv\")\n```\n\n\n```{r}\nESS_Subset$News_Split <- NA\n```\n\nCheck the ``ESS_Subset`` dataframe now! You should have an extra column ``News_Split`` at the end of the dataframe with all NA Values. \n\n\nWe can also set to this to any value we would like, for example instead of ``NA``, we can have all the cells take the value of 0.\n\n```{r}\nESS_Subset$News_Split <- 0\n```\n\n<div class=\"card text-white bg-dark mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 50rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Question</div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">What could be a use of first creating an empty column?</p>\n<p class=\"card-text\">If we are using the ``if()`` statement in R to create columns, then any row which does not fill the criteria already has some logical value. It also helps with avoiding type conflicts.  </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n\nIn today's class, we will look at three ways to split a column variable in R: \n\n<div class=\"accordion\" id=\"essExerciseAccordion\">\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepOne1\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseOne1\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseOne1\">\nMethod 1: Using  ``ifelse()``\n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseOne1\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepOne1\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\nThe ``ifelse()`` function is the quick way to split data. It can get quite messy in many cases. It works by evaluating a logical condition and returning one value if TRUE and another if FALSE. To handle multiple splits, you will have to \"nest\" the functions. \n\n<div class=\"card text-white bg-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 50rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Tip!</div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">In R, nesting an ifelse() simply means putting a second ifelse() statement inside the \"else\" (false) slot of the first one.</p>\n\nThe standard syntax for a single ``ifelse()`` is:\n``ifelse(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)``\n\nTo nest it, you replace value_if_false with a brand new ``ifelse()``:\n``ifelse(condition1, value1, ifelse(condition2, value2, value3))``\n\n\n\n</div>\n</div>\n\n**How can be use this command to split one of our columns?** Give it a try! Try splitting into 2 factors and 3 factors. \n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepTwo2\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseTwo2\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseTwo2\">\nMethod 2: Tidyverse with ``mutate()`` and ``case_when()`` \n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseTwo2\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepTwo2\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\n\nUsing tidyverse is cleaner and easy to read. ``case_when()`` is the cleaner, more powerful version of ``ifelse()``. It allows you to list multiple conditions sequentially without nesting! \n\nHere is an example: \n<img src=\"tidyverse_split.png\" alt=\"Tidyverse Splitting\" style=\"max-width:100%;\">\n\n<div class=\"alert alert-dismissible alert-danger\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn-close\" data-bs-dismiss=\"alert\"></button>\n  Can you spot a possible mistake in this code snipped?\n</div>\n\n\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"accordion-item\">\n<h2 class=\"accordion-header\" id=\"stepFour3\">\n<button class=\"accordion-button collapsed\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#collapseFour4\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"collapseFour4\">\nMethod 3: Using ``cut()``\n</button>\n</h2>\n<div id=\"collapseFour4\" class=\"accordion-collapse collapse\" aria-labelledby=\"stepFour3\" data-bs-parent=\"#essExerciseAccordion\">\n<div class=\"accordion-body\">\n\nThe ``cut()`` function is specifically designed for converting numeric vectors into factors by cutting them into intervals. This is often the best way to split the data which is separated into equal/unequal but consistant intervals. \n\nHere is an example: \n<img src=\"tidyverse2.png\" alt=\"using cut()\" style=\"max-width:100%;\">\n\n\n\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n\n<br>\n\nBased on this, let us attempt the first task for this week:\n\n**Tasks**:\n\n1. Load the ESS subset from last week\n\n2. Create a variable for political knowledge or news consumption (`nwspol`) — split into high/low\n\n3. Compare policy preferences (e.g., redistribution, immigration) between high- and low-information respondents\n\n4. `ggplot(ess, aes(x = imwbcnt, fill = news_group)) + geom_density(alpha = 0.5)`\n\n**Discussion questions**:\n\n- Are high- and low-information respondents very different? Or surprisingly similar?\n\n- If they're similar, what does that imply about shortcuts?\n\n- If they're different, is information the cause or is something else (education, income) driving both? \n\n## Task Update \n\nWe are now done with the following: \n\n<div class=\"row\">\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Exercise 1 Tasks</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nT1: Downloading and Loading ESS Subset on R. ✅<br>\nT2: Introduction to Key Commands ✅ <br>\nT3: Plotting Variable Distributions ✅  <br>\nT4: Cross Country and Mode Comparision ✅   \n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n\n<div class=\"row\">\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Exercise 2 Tasks</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nT1: Finishing Recap and Exercise 1 ✅<br>\nT2: Splitting Variable of Choice ✅<br>\nT3: Information Shortcut Task ✅<br>\nT4: Constructed preferences \n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\nLet us move to the final exercise task! \n\n<div class=\"alert alert-dismissible alert-danger\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn-close\" data-bs-dismiss=\"alert\"></button>\n  <strong>Wait!</strong> Let us pause here and ask any questions if something is unclear.\n</div>\n\n\n## Constructed preferences\n\n**Tasks**:\n\n1. Find two ESS items that measure \"the same thing\" differently. For example, immigration measured as economic impact (`imbgeco`) vs. cultural impact (`imueclt`) vs. general (\"make country better/worse\" `imwbcnt`)\n\n2. Correlate them: `cor(col1, col2)`\n\n3. Are they as correlated as you'd expect? Make a scatter plot.\n\n<div class=\"alert alert-dismissible alert-danger\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn-close\" data-bs-dismiss=\"alert\"></button>\n  <strong>We will do this task together! Let us discuss how to go about this in R!</strong>\n</div>\n\n\n**Discussion**:\n\n- If these all measure \"immigration attitudes,\" why aren't they perfectly correlated?\n\n- This connects back to Session 2 (Blankenberg, Bosshard): convergent validity failures\n\n- The way you frame the question shapes the answer\n\n## Independant Tasks \n\nNow, we can try the independent tasks for W1 and W2! I will also send an exercise email with the practice question as practicing is very important whenever learning a new programming language! \n\n<div class=\"col-sm-6\">\n<div class=\"card border-primary mb-3\" style=\"max-width: 30rem;\">\n<div class=\"card-header\">Independent Work</div>\n<div class=\"card-body text-primary\">\n<p class=\"card-text\">\nIT1: How does T3 change based on other variables available  <br>\nIT2: Survey Time and Sensitive Questions <br>\nIT3: Demographic, Country, Mode and Time Based Differences  <br>\nIT4: Testing all 3 Ways to Split a Column  <br>\nIT5: Plotting New Distributions With/Without the Split <br>\nIT6: Working on Exercise send via Moodle\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"knitr"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":true,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"lat